From 2bdfc447a7206d135ce215ce42b3049ca690cb08 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artem Hutsaliuk <artemgutsaliukv@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 23:03:33 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 153/194] arm64: dts: qcom: sm7150: add wakeup interrupt to the
 TX SoundWire controller

Capture traffic originates at the codec, so the microphone bus has to be
able to wake itself: unlike the RX bus, which is woken by whoever plays
audio, the TX controller needs the separate wakeup interrupt.

Without it the TX SoundWire device never leaves runtime suspend.
pm_runtime_resume_and_get() returns -EBUSY, regmap-irq floods the log
with "IRQ sync failed to resume", the audio service on the ADSP is
restarted and the sound card fails to instantiate with -EBUSY - taking
the speaker down with it.

The driver already supports this: qcom_swrm_probe() takes the wakeup
interrupt as the second one and the binding documents the names as
"core" and "wakeup". The interrupt number is GIC_SPI 500, taken from the
vendor device tree, where the TX controller is the only one declared
wakeup capable.

Tested on a Xiaomi Mi 9T (SM7150-AA, WCD9375): the sixteen consecutive
"IRQ sync failed to resume" messages are gone and the card comes up on
every boot.

Signed-off-by: Artem Hutsaliuk <artemgutsaliukv@gmail.com>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm7150.dtsi | 13 ++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm7150.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm7150.dtsi
index 2ee0d36db..b30ad8b74 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm7150.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm7150.dtsi
@@ -4695,7 +4695,18 @@ swr2: soundwire-controller@62ed0000 {
 			reg = <0 0x62ed0000 0 0x2000>;
 			compatible = "qcom,soundwire-v1.5.1";
 			status = "disabled";
-			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 296 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+			/*
+			 * The TX bus needs the second, wakeup interrupt: capture
+			 * traffic originates at the codec, so the bus has to wake
+			 * itself. Without it the SoundWire device never leaves
+			 * runtime suspend, regmap-irq keeps reporting "IRQ sync
+			 * failed to resume" and the sound card fails to probe.
+			 * The RX bus does not need it - it is woken by the
+			 * playback side.
+			 */
+			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 296 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>,
+				     <GIC_SPI 500 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+			interrupt-names = "core", "wakeup";
 
 			clocks = <&txmacro>;
 			clock-names = "iface";
